        "findings": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change slots into the existing structure cleanly and idiomatically:\n\n- `applyRiskFlag` is a direct sibling of `applyBulkSignupWarning` (915) and `applyCapacityWarning` (929): same `apply*(OwnerDto)` shape, same \"set one flag on the response DTO\" responsibility, inserted at the same three call sites as the existing pair. A maintainer would find this exactly where they'd expect it.\n- `isDisposableAdjacentEmail` is a well-scoped, cohesive helper placed next to its caller, reusing the existing `DISPOSABLE_EMAIL_DOMAINS`, `isBlank`, and `Locale.ROOT` conventions already used by `normalizeEmail` (689). It correctly does *not* fold itself into `normalizeEmail`, since that method is a creation-time validator that rejects, whereas this is a read-time classifier \u2014 keeping them separate is the right call.\n- The OpenAPI addition mirrors the adjacent `readOnly` boolean flags (`possibleDuplicate`, `capacityWarning`) in placement and style.\n\nOn the one thing that might look like a smell \u2014 `applyRiskFlag` recomputing `countOwnersInCity(ownerDto.getCity())` which `applyCapacityWarning` also computes one line earlier \u2014 this is actually the established idiom of this class, not a regression. Every `apply*`/`reject*`/`count*` helper here re-derives its inputs from `findAllOwners()` independently and self-contained (e.g. `applyCapacityWarning` and `applyBulkSignupWarning` share no state; `rejectCityAtCapacity` and `applyCapacityWarning` both call `countOwnersInCity` separately). Introducing shared intermediate state just for `riskFlag` would make it the odd one out. Keeping each flag self-contained is the more coherent choice given the surrounding code.\n\nNo structural changes needed."
